Write a brief that the rewriting steps will work from. The rewrite is free to change how this prompt is expressed but has to keep everything it actually requires, so the brief's job is to make those requirements explicit enough that a later revision can be checked against them.

Cover, in this order:

Task — one or two sentences on what this prompt asks a model to do, and what a good response to it looks like.

Requirements — every requirement and constraint the prompt expresses, one per line, numbered R1, R2, and so on. Include the ones it only implies: an output format its wording takes for granted, a tone, a length, an audience, something it rules out. Where the prompt is ambiguous, say so and give the reading you think was intended. Later steps treat this list as settled, so a flagged uncertainty is worth far more than a confident guess.

Inputs — each placeholder, in the {$NAME} form, what it holds, and where the prompt uses it.

Examples — each worked example the prompt contains, what it demonstrates, and whether it is there to fix an output format, a style, or an edge case. Write "none" if the prompt has no examples.

Weaknesses — what is unclear, redundant, contradictory, or likely to produce inconsistent results, each with the specific text at fault.

Put the whole brief in a single <brief> block and start your response with it.
